    "instruction": "You need to design a game that can be played by exactly 5 people simultaneously, requires no special equipment (only common household items), takes 15-20 minutes to complete, involves both cooperation and competition, and ensures that all players remain actively engaged throughout (no elimination or sitting out). The game should be easy to explain but have enough strategic depth to remain interesting over multiple plays. Describe the complete rules, objective, and mechanics of this game.",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>You need to design a game that can be played by exactly 5 people simultaneously, requires no special equipment (only common household items), takes 15-20 minutes to complete, involves both cooperation and competition, and ensures that all players remain actively engaged throughout (no elimination or sitting out). The game should be easy to explain but have enough strategic depth to remain interesting over multiple plays. Describe the complete rules, objective, and mechanics of this game.<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "Okay, so I need to design a game that can be played by exactly 5 people at the same time. It should be done in 15-20 minutes, using only common household items. It needs to involve both cooperation and competition, and keep everyone engaged without anyone sitting out. The game should be easy to explain but have enough strategy to keep things interesting over multiple plays.\n\nFirst, I should think about what 5 people can do in 15-20 minutes. Maybe something quick but requires some planning. Maybe a team-building activity? Or perhaps a puzzle that requires teamwork. Alternatively, a game that can be set up with items like cards, dice, or maybe even a timer.\n\nLet me think about the elements. Since it needs cooperation and competition, maybe a cooperative puzzle where everyone contributes, but also a competitive element where they have to outdo each other. But how to do that without elimination.\n\nWait, maybe a card game where each player has a set of cards, and they have to work together to play them, but also have to compete in some way. Or perhaps a cooperative board game where each player contributes to the board, but also has to compete in some way.\n\nAnother idea: maybe a relay race, but with a twist where each person has to contribute to the team's progress, but also have to compete against others. But that might be too time-consuming.\n\nWait, maybe a memory game where each player has a set of cards, and they have to match them with others. But that might be too simple and not involve much strategy.\n\nAlternatively, a team-based card game where each player has a set of cards, and they have to work together to play them, but also have to compete in some way. For example, each player has a certain number of points, and they have to accumulate points by playing certain cards, but also have to compete against each other in some way, like who can play the most cards in a certain time.\n\nWait, but the user mentioned that the game should be easy t