er of the hallway's orientation. If the hallway is viewed from a different angle, perhaps the length appears longer. But the hallway is straight, so unless it's viewed at an angle, the length should remain the same.\n\nWait, hold on. Maybe the hallway is not straight in the way we perceive it because of the way it's constructed. For example, if the hallway is a straight line in space, but when viewed from a certain angle, it appears to be curved or something else.\n\nBut again, the problem says it's a straight hallway, so that's not it.\n\nWait, another thought: perhaps the hallway is not straight in the way we perceive it because of the way the ceiling is angled. For example, if the ceiling",
