ng by 6, then 14... Not sure if that helps. Maybe I should look at the denominators themselves.\n\nWait, 2, 5, 12, 29, 70. Let me see if these can be expressed in terms of powers of 2 or something similar.\n\n2 is 2.\n\n5 is 4 + 1.\n\n12 is 8 + 4.\n\n29 is 24 + 5.\n\n70 is 64 + 6.\n\nWait, that might not be helpful. Alternatively, let me see if the denominators can be expressed as 2^n + something.\n\n2 = 2^1 + 0\n\n5 = 2^2 + 1\n\n12 = 2^3 + 4\n\n29 = 2^4 + 5\n\n70 = 2^6 + 6\n\nHmm, not a clear pattern. Maybe another approach.\n\nWait, let me think about the numerators and denominators separately. Maybe the denominators are related to the numerators in some way.\n\nLooking at the numerators: 1, 3, 7, 15, 31. These are one less than powers of 2: 2^1 -1 =1, 2^2 -1=3, 2^3 -1=7, 2",
