  {
    "task_id": "PROB_111",
    "base_task_id": "PROB_111",
    "category": "probabilistic_thinking",
    "instruction": "An online platform runs A/B tests for a new feature. In the control group (50% of users), the conversion rate is 8%. In the test group (50% of users), the observed conversion rate is 9.2%. However, the platform knows that 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see, while regular users in the control group convert at 5%. A user converts after seeing the test version. What is the probability this user is a power user versus a regular user? Additionally, what must be the conversion rate of regular users in the test group to produce the observed 9.2% overall conversion rate?",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>An online platform runs A/B tests for a new feature. In the control group (50% of users), the conversion rate is 8%. In the test group (50% of users), the observed conversion rate is 9.2%. However, the platform knows that 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see, while regular users in the control group convert at 5%. A user converts after seeing the test version. What is the probability this user is a power user versus a regular user? Additionally, what must be the conversion rate of regular users in the test group to produce the observed 9.2% overall conversion rate?<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "Okay, so I have this problem about A/B testing on an online platform. There are two groups: a control group and a test group, each with 50% of users. The conversion rates are given, but there's some complexity because of power users who convert at 25% regardless of the version they see. I need to figure out two things: first, given that a user converted after seeing the test version, what's the probability they're a power user versus a regular user? Second, what should the conversion rate of regular users in the test group be so that the overall conversion rate is 9.2%? Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n\nStarting with the first part: given that a user converted after seeing the test version, what's the probability they're a power user or a regular user. I think this is a conditional probability problem, so I might need to use Bayes' theorem here. Let me recall Bayes' theorem: P(A|B) = P(B|A) * P(A) / P(B). So, in this case, I need to find P(Power User | Converted) and P(Regular User | Converted). \n\nFirst, let's define the events:\n\n- Let P be the event that a user is a power user.\n- Let R be the event that a user is a regular user.\n- Let C be the event that a user converts.\n\nWe know that P(P) = 15% = 0.15, and P(R) = 85% = 0.85 because 100% - 15% = 85%. \n\nNow, the power users convert at 25% regardless of the version, so P(C|P) = 0.25. For regular users in the control group, it's 5%, so P(C|R) = 0.05. But wait, the test group is also 50% regular users, so I think the regular users in the test group also have a 5% conversion rate? Or is it different? Hmm, the problem says \"regular users in the control group convert at 5%.\" It doesn't specify for the test group, so maybe the test group's regular users also have 5%? Or is it different? Hmm, the problem doesn't specify, so I might have to assume that the test group's regular users also have 5% conversion rate. So, P(C|R_test) = 0.05.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"the platform knows that 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see, while regular users in the control group convert at 5%.\" So, does that mean that in the test group, regular users also convert at 5%? Or is the test group's regular users different? Hmm, the wording is a bit ambiguous. It says \"regular users in the control group,\" so maybe the test group's regular users are also 5%. So, I think I can proceed with that assumption.\n\nSo, now, let's compute the overall probability of conversion, P(C). Since the platform is splitting users into control and test groups, each with 50% of users, but within each group, 15% are power users and 85% are regular users. So, the total conversion rate would be:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R) + P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nWait, no, that's not quite right. Because the test group is a separate group, so actually, the overall conversion rate is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut wait, no, because the test group is a separate group, so actually, the overall conversion rate is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut since the test group is 50% of the users, and the control group is 50%, but within each group, 15% are power users and 85% are regular users. So, actually, the overall P(C) is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut wait, no, because the test group is a separate group, so actually, the overall P(C) is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut since the test group is 50% of the users, and the control group is 50%, but",
    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>An online platform runs A/B tests for a new feature. In the control group (50% of users), the conversion rate is 8%. In the test group (50% of users), the observed conversion rate is 9.2%. However, the platform knows that 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see, while regular users in the control group convert at 5%. A user converts after seeing the test version. What is the probability this user is a power user versus a regular user? Additionally, what must be the conversion rate of regular users in the test group to produce the observed 9.2% overall conversion rate?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nOkay, so I have this problem about A/B testing on an online platform. There are two groups: a control group and a test group, each with 50% of users. The conversion rates are given, but there's some complexity because of power users who convert at 25% regardless of the version they see. I need to figure out two things: first, given that a user converted after seeing the test version, what's the probability they're a power user versus a regular user? Second, what should the conversion rate of regular users in the test group be so that the overall conversion rate is 9.2%? Hmm, let me break this down step by step.\n\nStarting with the first part: given that a user converted after seeing the test version, what's the probability they're a power user or a regular user. I think this is a conditional probability problem, so I might need to use Bayes' theorem here. Let me recall Bayes' theorem: P(A|B) = P(B|A) * P(A) / P(B). So, in this case, I need to find P(Power User | Converted) and P(Regular User | Converted). \n\nFirst, let's define the events:\n\n- Let P be the event that a user is a power user.\n- Let R be the event that a user is a regular user.\n- Let C be the event that a user converts.\n\nWe know that P(P) = 15% = 0.15, and P(R) = 85% = 0.85 because 100% - 15% = 85%. \n\nNow, the power users convert at 25% regardless of the version, so P(C|P) = 0.25. For regular users in the control group, it's 5%, so P(C|R) = 0.05. But wait, the test group is also 50% regular users, so I think the regular users in the test group also have a 5% conversion rate? Or is it different? Hmm, the problem says \"regular users in the control group convert at 5%.\" It doesn't specify for the test group, so maybe the test group's regular users also have 5%? Or is it different? Hmm, the problem doesn't specify, so I might have to assume that the test group's regular users also have 5% conversion rate. So, P(C|R_test) = 0.05.\n\nWait, but the problem says \"the platform knows that 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see, while regular users in the control group convert at 5%.\" So, does that mean that in the test group, regular users also convert at 5%? Or is the test group's regular users different? Hmm, the wording is a bit ambiguous. It says \"regular users in the control group,\" so maybe the test group's regular users are also 5%. So, I think I can proceed with that assumption.\n\nSo, now, let's compute the overall probability of conversion, P(C). Since the platform is splitting users into control and test groups, each with 50% of users, but within each group, 15% are power users and 85% are regular users. So, the total conversion rate would be:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R) + P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nWait, no, that's not quite right. Because the test group is a separate group, so actually, the overall conversion rate is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut wait, no, because the test group is a separate group, so actually, the overall conversion rate is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut since the test group is 50% of the users, and the control group is 50%, but within each group, 15% are power users and 85% are regular users. So, actually, the overall P(C) is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut wait, no, because the test group is a separate group, so actually, the overall P(C) is:\n\nP(C) = P(P) * P(C|P) + P(R) * P(C|R)\n\nBut since the test group is 50% of the users, and the control group is 50%, but",
