  {
    "task_id": "PROB_112",
    "base_task_id": "PROB_112",
    "category": "probabilistic_thinking",
    "instruction": "A quality control system inspects electronic components in three sequential stages. Stage 1 catches 80% of defective items but also incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. Items that pass Stage 1 go to Stage 2, which catches 70% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. Items passing Stage 2 go to Stage 3, which catches 60% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. If the initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, and an item makes it through all three stages to the customer, what is the probability it is actually defective? Also, what percentage of originally good items are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process?",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A quality control system inspects electronic components in three sequential stages. Stage 1 catches 80% of defective items but also incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. Items that pass Stage 1 go to Stage 2, which catches 70% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. Items passing Stage 2 go to Stage 3, which catches 60% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. If the initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, and an item makes it through all three stages to the customer, what is the probability it is actually defective? Also, what percentage of originally good items are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process?<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "Okay, so I have this quality control problem to solve. It involves three stages, and I need to find two probabilities: the probability that an item that makes it through all three stages is actually defective, and the percentage of originally good items that are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process. Hmm, let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me understand the problem. There are three stages: Stage 1, Stage 2, and Stage 3. Each stage has a certain rate of catching defective items and incorrectly rejecting good items. The initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, so that's the probability that a randomly selected item is defective before any stages are applied.\n\nAn item that makes it through all three stages to the customer must have passed all three stages without being rejected. So, I need to figure out the probability that an item is defective given that it passed all three stages. That sounds like a conditional probability problem, so maybe Bayes' theorem will come into play here.\n\nLet me define some variables to make this clearer. Let me denote:\n\n- D: The event that an item is defective.\n- G: The event that an item is good.\n- S1: The event that an item passes Stage 1.\n- S2: The event that an item passes Stage 2.\n- S3: The event that an item passes Stage 3.\n\nWe are given that the initial defect rate is 10%, so P(D) = 0.10 and P(G) = 0.90.\n\nNow, let's look at each stage and the probabilities of catching defective items and incorrectly rejecting good items.\n\nStage 1:\n- It catches 80% of defective items. So, if an item is defective, the probability it passes Stage 1 is 80%, or 0.80.\n- It incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. So, if an item is good, the probability it fails Stage 1 is 5%, or 0.05.\n\nStage 2:\n- It catches 70% of remaining defective items. So, if an item passes Stage 1, it has a 70% chance of passing Stage 2.\n- It incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. So, if an item passes Stage 1, the probability it fails Stage 2 is 3%, or 0.03.\n\nStage 3:\n- It catches 60% of remaining defective items. So, if an item passes Stage 2, it has a 60% chance of passing Stage 3.\n- It incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. So, if an item passes Stage 2, the probability it fails Stage 3 is 2%, or 0.02.\n\nWe need to find two things:\n\n1. The probability that an item is defective given that it passed all three stages, i.e., P(D | S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3).\n2. The percentage of originally good items that are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process, i.e., P(Fail Stage 3 | G).\n\nLet me tackle the first part first.\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem, P(D | S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3) = P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | D) * P(D) / P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3)\n\nBut since we know the probabilities for each stage, we can compute P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | D) as the product of the probabilities of passing each stage given that the item is defective.\n\nSimilarly, P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3) is the total probability that an item passes all three stages, regardless of whether it's defective or good. So, we can compute this as P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3) = P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | D) * P(D) + P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | G) * P(G)\n\nBut since we need P(D | S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3), we can compute it as [P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | D) * P(D)] / [P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | D) * P(D) + P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | G) * P(G)]\n\nSo, let's compute each part.\n\nFirst, compute P(S1 ∩ S2 ∩ S3 | D):\n\nStage 1: P(S1 | D) = 0.80\nStage 2: P(S2 | S1) = 0.70\nStage 3: P(S3 |",
