    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A climate model predicts severe storms based on four independent atmospheric conditions: high humidity (occurs 30% of days), low pressure (occurs 25% of days), high wind shear (occurs 20% of days), and temperature inversion (occurs 15% of days). Historical data shows that when all four conditions are present, severe storms occur 95% of the time. When exactly three conditions are present, storms occur 60% of the time. When exactly two conditions are present, storms occur 25% of the time. When fewer than two conditions are present, storms occur 5% of the time. On a given day, a severe storm occurs. What is the probability that all four atmospheric conditions were present? What is the probability that exactly three conditions were present?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nOkay, so I have this probability problem here about a climate model predicting severe storms based on four atmospheric conditions. The question is asking for the probability that all four conditions were present given that a severe storm occurred, and also the probability that exactly three conditions were present given that a severe storm occurred. Hmm, this sounds like a conditional probability problem. I remember that conditional probability is about the probability of an event given that another event has occurred. The formula for conditional probability is P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B). So, in this case, I need to find P(all four conditions | storm) and P(exactly three conditions | storm).\n\nFirst, let me parse the information given. The model predicts four independent atmospheric conditions: high humidity (H) which occurs 30% of the time, low pressure (L) which occurs 25% of the time, high wind shear (W) which occurs 20% of the time, and temperature inversion (I) which occurs 15% of the time. So, each of these conditions has a certain probability of occurring on any given day.\n\nNow, the problem also gives the probability of severe storms based on how many conditions are present. It says that when all four conditions are present, severe storms occur 95% of the time. When exactly three conditions are present, storms occur 60% of the time. When exactly two conditions are present, storms occur 25% of the time. And when fewer than two conditions are present, storms occur 5% of the time.\n\nSo, I think I need to model this as a probability distribution over the number of conditions present. Let me denote the number of conditions present as k, where k can be 0, 1, 2, 3, or 4. The probabilities for each k are given by the problem, but I need to verify if they are independent or not. Wait, the problem says the conditions are independent, so the probability of having exactly k conditions present is the sum of the probabilities of each individual condition occurring or not occurring, depending on k.\n\nWait, actually, no. Since the conditions are independent, the probability of having exactly k conditions present is the sum of the probabilities of each combination of k conditions occurring and the remaining (4 - k) conditions not occurring. But since each condition is independent, the probability of exactly k conditions is C(4, k) * (p_k) * (1 - p_{not k}), where p_k is the probability of condition k occurring, and 1 - p_{not k} is the probability of condition k not occurring.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I need to clarify. The problem says that each condition occurs with a certain probability, but it doesn't specify whether the conditions are mutually exclusive or not. Hmm, actually, the problem says \"four independent atmospheric conditions,\" so I think that means each condition is independent of the others, but they don't necessarily have to be mutually exclusive. So, the probability of having exactly k conditions present is the sum over all combinations of k conditions of the product of their probabilities.\n\nBut wait, actually, no. If the conditions are independent, the probability of exactly k conditions occurring is the sum over all subsets of size k of the product of the probabilities of each condition in the subset and the product of the probabilities of the conditions not in the subset. But that seems complicated because it's a lot of terms.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let me think again. The problem gives the probability of severe storms based on the number of conditions present. So, perhaps the probability of a storm is a function of the number of conditions present. So, maybe the probability of a storm is given as:\n\nP(storm) = P(storm | 4 conditions) * P(4 conditions) + P(storm | 3 conditions) * P(3 conditions) + P(storm | 2 conditions) * P(2 conditions) + P(storm | 1 condition) * P(1 condition) + P(storm | 0 conditions) * P(0 conditions)\n\nBut wait, the problem doesn't give me the probabilities of having exactly 0, 1, 2, 3, or 4 conditions. It only gives me the probabilities of having all four, exactly three, exactly two, or fewer than two conditions. So, I need to figure out the probabilities of having exactly 0, 1, 2, 3, or 4 conditions.\n\nSince the conditions are independent, the probability of having exactly k conditions present is equal to the sum over all combinations of k conditions of the product of their probabilities. But since each condition is independent, the probability of having exactly k conditions is C(4, k) * (p_k) * (1 - p_{not k",
