    "chain_full": "Okay, so I have this casino game problem to solve. Let me try to understand it step by step. \n\nFirst, the setup: there's an opaque bag with 8 gold tokens, 6 silver tokens, and 4 bronze tokens. The game is played in rounds where you draw one token randomly. Depending on what you draw, you either win some money and return the token along with additional tokens, or you win nothing and remove the token. The game ends when you draw your third bronze token. \n\nThe questions are: \n1. What is the probability that you draw at least one gold token before drawing your second bronze token?\n2. What is the expected number of silver tokens in the bag when the game ends?\n\nLet me tackle the first question first.\n\n**Problem 1: Probability of drawing at least one gold token before drawing the second bronze token**\n\nHmm, so we need the probability that before getting our second bronze token, we have drawn at least one gold token. That means, in the process of drawing tokens until we get our third bronze, we must have drawn at least one gold token in the first two draws. \n\nWait, actually, no. Because the game ends when we draw the third bronze. So, the game could end on the third, fourth, fifth, etc., draw. But we need the probability that before the third bronze is drawn, we have at least one gold token. So, in other words, in the first two draws, we have at least one gold token, and then the third bronze is drawn on the third, fourth, etc., draw.\n\nAlternatively, another way to think about it is: what is the probability that in the first two draws, we have at least one gold token, and then the third bronze is drawn on the third draw or later.\n\nBut maybe it's easier to model this as a negative binomial-like problem, but with some modifications because of the token returns.\n\nWait, but the token returns complicate things because each draw affects the composition of the bag. So, the probabilities change as we draw tokens.\n\nLet me think about the possible states. The game can be in different states based on the number of gold, silver, and bronze tokens remaining. But since the game ends when we draw the third bronze, we can model this as a Markov chain where each state is defined by the number of gold, silver, and bronze tokens remaining, and the number of times we've drawn a bronze token so far.\n\nBut that might get complicated. Maybe there's a smarter way.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can model this as a probability of having at least one gold token before the second bronze. So, the game could end on the third draw, fourth draw, etc., but we need the probability that in the first two draws, we have at least one gold, and then the third is bronze.\n\nWait, no. Because the game could end on the third draw, but if we have already drawn a gold in the first two, then the third could be bronze or not. Hmm, maybe it's better to think in terms of the possible number of gold tokens drawn before the second bronze.\n\nWait, perhaps it's better to model this as a probability of having at least one gold token in the first two draws, and then the third draw is bronze. But actually, the game could end on the third draw, so if we have already drawn a gold in the first two, the third could be bronze or not. So, the probability that we have at least one gold before the second bronze is equal to the probability that in the first two draws, we have at least one gold, and then the third draw is bronze, plus the probability that in the first two draws, we have zero golds, and then the third draw is bronze, but in that case, we have zero golds, so it doesn't satisfy the condition.\n\nWait, no. The condition is that we draw at least one gold before drawing the second bronze. So, if we have zero golds in the first two draws, then the third draw is bronze, but we didn't draw any golds before the second bronze. So, that doesn't satisfy the condition. Therefore, the probability we need is the probability that in the first two draws, we have at least one gold, and then the third draw is bronze.\n\nBut actually, the game could end on the third draw, so if we have at least one gold in the first two draws, the third draw could be bronze or not. So, the probability that we have at least one gold before the second bronze is equal to the probability that in the first two draws, we have at least one gold, and then the third draw is bronze.\n\nBut wait, no. Because the game could end on the third draw, so if we have at least one gold in the first two draws, the game could end on the third draw",
