      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "So, I need to calculate P(D) = P(D|A)*P(A) + P(D|B)*P(B) + P(D|C)*P(C).\n\nGiven:\n\n- P(A) = 25% = 0.25\n- P(B) = 40% = 0.4\n- P(C) = 35% = 0.35\n\nAnd the success rates:\n\n- P(D|A) = 90% = 0.9\n- P(D|B) = 75% = 0.75\n- P(D|C) = 80% = 0.8\n\nSo, plugging in the numbers:\n\nP(D) = (0.9 * 0.25) + (0.75 * 0.4) + (0.8 * 0.35)\n\nLet me compute each term:\n\n0.9 * 0.25 = 0.225\n\n0.75 * 0.4 = 0.3\n\n0.8 * 0.35 = 0.28\n\nAdding them up: 0.225 + 0.3 + 0.28 = 0.805\n\nSo, P(D) = 0.805\n\nTherefore, applying Bayes' Theorem:\n\nP(B|D) = (0.75 * 0.4) / 0.805\n\nCompute numerator: 0.75 * 0.4 = 0.3\n\nSo, P(B|D) = 0.3 / 0.805 ≈ 0.3725 or approximately 37.25%\n\nSo, the probability that the bird migrated to Region B given complete tracking data is about 37.25%."
      },
      {
        "label": "example-testing",
        "text": "Wait, let me double-check the calculations:\n\n0.9 * 0.25 = 0.225\n\n0.75 * 0.4 = 0.3\n\n0.8 * 0.35 = 0.28\n\nTotal P(D) = 0.225 + 0.3 + 0.28 = 0.805\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nThen, P(B|D) = (0.75 * 0.4) / 0.805 = 0.3 / 0.805 ≈ 0.3725\n\nSo, approximately 37.25%. That seems reasonable."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Now, moving on to the second question: If the institute tagged 200 birds, how many birds would have complete tracking data?"
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Well, the total number of birds is 200. The probability that a single bird's tracking data is complete is P"
      }
