    "category": "spatial_reasoning",
    "instruction": "Five identical cubes, each with edge length 4 cm, are arranged to form a three-dimensional cross shape: one central cube with four cubes attached to four of its faces (top, bottom, front, and back), leaving the left and right faces exposed. This structure is then dipped into red paint and removed. After the paint dries, the structure is disassembled into the five separate cubes. For each of the five cubes, determine how many faces have paint on them, and calculate the total surface area of all unpainted faces across all five cubes.",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>Five identical cubes, each with edge length 4 cm, are arranged to form a three-dimensional cross shape: one central cube with four cubes attached to four of its faces (top, bottom, front, and back), leaving the left and right faces exposed. This structure is then dipped into red paint and removed. After the paint dries, the structure is disassembled into the five separate cubes. For each of the five cubes, determine how many faces have paint on them, and calculate the total surface area of all unpainted faces across all five cubes.<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "Okay, so I have this problem where there are five identical cubes, each with an edge length of 4 cm. They're arranged in a three-dimensional cross shape. That means there's one central cube, and then four other cubes attached to each of its four faces: top, bottom, front, and back. The left and right faces are left exposed, so they don't have any paint on them. Then, the entire structure is dipped in red paint, and when it dries, it's disassembled into five separate cubes. For each cube, I need to figure out how many faces are painted, and then calculate the total surface area of all the unpainted faces across all five cubes.\n\nAlright, let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's visualize the structure. There's a central cube, and on each of its four faces (top, bottom, front, back), there's another cube attached. So, the front cube is attached to the front face of the central cube, the back cube to the back face, the top cube to the top face, and the bottom cube to the bottom face. The left and right faces of the central cube are still exposed, meaning they don't have any paint on them.\n\nNow, when the entire structure is dipped in red paint, all the outer surfaces get painted. After drying, the structure is disassembled into five separate cubes. So, each cube is now separate, and we need to figure out how many faces of each cube are painted and then calculate the total unpainted surface area.\n\nLet me start by considering each cube individually.\n\n1. **Central Cube:**\n   - The central cube is attached to four other cubes, one on each of its top, bottom, front, and back faces.\n   - Since it's attached to four other cubes, the central cube is only connected to these four. So, the top, bottom, front, and back faces of the central cube are covered by the attached cubes and thus not exposed.\n   - The left and right faces of the central cube are still exposed because they are not connected to any other cube.\n   - So, the central cube has 2 painted faces (left and right) and 4 unpainted faces (top, bottom, front, back).\n   - Wait, hold on. Each cube has 6 faces. If the central cube is connected to four others, does that mean all four of its faces are covered? So, the top, bottom, front, and back are covered, leaving the left and right faces exposed. So, yes, 2 painted faces and 4 unpainted.\n\n2. **Front Cube:**\n   - The front cube is attached to the front face of the central cube.\n   - So, the front face of the front cube is attached and thus not exposed.\n   - The front cube is also connected to the central cube, so the top, bottom, left, and right faces of the front cube are exposed.\n   - The front cube's top, bottom, left, and right faces are painted, so that's 4 painted faces.\n   - The front cube's top, bottom, left, and right are painted, but the front face is attached, so it's not exposed. So, 4 painted faces and 1 unpainted face (the front face).\n\n3. **Back Cube:**\n   - Similar to the front cube, the back cube is attached to the back face of the central cube.\n   - So, the back face of the back cube is attached and not exposed.\n   - The back cube's top, bottom, left, and right faces are exposed, so 4 painted faces.\n   - The back cube's top, bottom, left, and right are painted, and the back face is attached, so 4 painted faces and 1 unpainted face.\n\n4. **Top Cube:**\n   - The top cube is attached to the top face of the central cube.\n   - So, the top face of the top cube is attached and not exposed.\n   - The top cube's bottom, left, right, and front faces are exposed, so 4 painted faces.\n   - The top cube's bottom, left, right, and front are painted, and the top face is attached, so 4 painted faces and 1 unpainted face.\n\n5. **Bottom Cube:**\n   - Similar to the top cube, the bottom cube is attached to the bottom face of the central cube.\n   - So, the bottom face of the bottom cube is attached and not exposed.\n   - The bottom cube's top, left, right, and front faces are exposed, so 4 painted faces.\n   - The bottom cube's top, left, right, and front are painted, and the bottom face is attached, so 4 painted faces and 1 unpainted face.\n\nWait, hold on. Let",
