    "chain_full": "Alright, so I've got this logic puzzle here with four colleagues: Priya, Quinn, Rafael, and Sophia. Each of them makes a statement about who will lead the project and who will be deputy lead. The catch is that exactly one person's statement is false, and the others are true. I need to figure out who is lying, who leads, and who serves as deputy lead.\n\nLet me start by writing down each person's statement to have a clear view.\n\n1. **Priya's statement**: \"If Quinn leads the project, then Rafael will not be the lead, and Sophia will serve as deputy lead.\"\n2. **Quinn's statement**: \"Either I will lead the project or Sophia will, but not both of us.\"\n3. **Rafael's statement**: \"If Priya's statement is true, then I am available to serve as deputy lead.\"\n4. **Sophia's statement**: \"Quinn's statement is false, and exactly two of us will have leadership roles.\"\n\nOkay, so each of these statements is a conditional or a conjunction. Since exactly one statement is false, the rest must be true. That means three of the four statements are true, and one is false. My job is to figure out which one is false and then deduce the roles based on the true statements.\n\nLet me break down each statement and see how they relate to each other.\n\nStarting with **Quinn's statement**: \"Either I will lead the project or Sophia will, but not both of us.\" So, this is an exclusive OR (XOR) between Quinn and Sophia. That means exactly one of them is leading, and the other is deputy lead. So, either Quinn leads and Sophia is deputy, or vice versa.\n\n**Rafael's statement** is a bit more complex: \"If Priya's statement is true, then I am available to serve as deputy lead.\" So, if Priya's statement is true, then Rafael is available to be deputy lead. But if Priya's statement is false, then this implication is automatically true, regardless of Rafael's status.\n\n**Priya's statement** is a bit more involved: \"If Quinn leads the project, then Rafael will not be the