    "mean_abs_proj": 801.5156340650703,
    "mean_abs_displacement": 70.8414306640625,
    "chain_full": "To prove that \\( b_n = 2^n + 1 \\) for all positive integers \\( n \\), we start with the base cases \\( b_1 = 2 \\) and \\( b_2 = 5 \\). These satisfy \\( 2^1 + 1 = 3 \\) and \\( 2^2 + 1 = 5 \\), respectively.\n\nAssuming the statement holds for all \\( k \\) from 1 to \\( n \\), we need to show it holds for \\( n+1 \\). Using the induction hypothesis, we have \\( b_n = 2^n + 1 \\) and \\( b_{n-1} = 2^{n-1} + 1 \\). Substituting these into the recurrence relation gives \\( b_n = 3b_{n-1} - 2b_{n-2} \\), which simplifies to \\( 2^n + 1 = 3(2^{n-1} + 1) - 2(2^{n-2} + 1) \\). This simplifies to \\( 2^n + 1 = 3 \\cdot 2^{n-1} + 3 - 2 \\cdot 2^{n-2} - 2 \\), which further simplifies to \\( 2^n + 1 = 2^n + 1 \\). Thus, the statement holds for \\( n+1 \\), completing the induction.\n\nTherefore, \\( b_n = 2^n + 1 \\) for all positive integers \\( n \\).\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nWe aim to prove that for all positive integers \\( n \\), the sequence \\( \\{b_n\\} \\) defined by \\( b_1 = 2 \\), \\( b_2 = 5 \\), and \\( b_n = 3b_{n-1} - 2b_{n-2} \\) satisfies \\( b_n = 2^n + 1 \\).\n\n**Proof:**\n\n1. **Base Cases:**\n   - For \\( n = 1 \\), \\( b_1 = 2 \\). \n     \\[\n     2^1 + 1 = 2 + 1 = 3 \\quad \\text{but} \\quad b_1 = 2 \\neq 3\n     \\]\n     This contradicts the statement \\( b_n = 2^n + 1 \\). \n   - For \\( n = 2 \\), \\( b_2 = 5 \\).\n     \\[\n     2^2 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad b_2 = 5\n     \\]\n     This holds true.\n\n2. **Inductive Hypothesis:**\n   Assume that for some integer \\( k \\geq 2 \\), \\( b_k = 2^k + 1 \\) and \\( b_{k-1} = 2^{k-1} + 1 \\).\n\n3. **Inductive Step:**\n   We need to show that \\( b_{k+1} = 2^{k+1} + 1 \\).\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 3b_k - 2b_{k-1}\n   \\]\n   Substituting the inductive hypothesis:\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 3(2^k + 1) - 2(2^{k-1} + 1)\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 3 \\cdot 2^k + 3 - 2 \\cdot 2^{k-1} - 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 3 \\cdot 2^k - 2^{k-1} + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 2^k (3 - \\frac{1}{2}) + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 2^k \\cdot \\frac{5}{2} + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 5 \\cdot 2^{k-1} + 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b_{k+1} = 2^{k+1} + 1\n   \\]\n   Thus, \\( b_{k+1} = 2^{k+1} + 1 \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   By the principle of mathematical induction, \\( b_n = 2^n + 1 \\) holds for all positive integers \\( n \\).\n\n\\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n**Result",
