  {
    "task_id": "PATT_110",
    "base_task_id": "PATT_110",
    "category": "pattern_recognition",
    "instruction": "Consider a sequence where each term is a matrix transformation result. The sequence of 2×2 matrices follows this pattern:\nMatrix 1: [[1,2],[3,4]] with determinant -2\nMatrix 2: [[2,5],[7,12]] with determinant -11\nMatrix 3: [[5,12],[17,29]] with determinant -59\nMatrix 4: [[12,29],[41,70]] with determinant -349\nEach matrix's elements follow specific relationships to the previous matrix, and the determinants form their own sequence. Identify the pattern governing both the matrix element generation and the determinant sequence, then determine what Matrix 5 should be and verify its determinant fits the pattern.",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>Consider a sequence where each term is a matrix transformation result. The sequence of 2×2 matrices follows this pattern:\nMatrix 1: [[1,2],[3,4]] with determinant -2\nMatrix 2: [[2,5],[7,12]] with determinant -11\nMatrix 3: [[5,12],[17,29]] with determinant -59\nMatrix 4: [[12,29],[41,70]] with determinant -349\nEach matrix's elements follow specific relationships to the previous matrix, and the determinants form their own sequence. Identify the pattern governing both the matrix element generation and the determinant sequence, then determine what Matrix 5 should be and verify its determinant fits the pattern.<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "To determine the pattern for Matrix 5, we observe that each subsequent matrix has elements generated by doubling the previous matrix and adding the preceding matrix. This results in Matrix 5 having elements [12, 29] and [41, 70]. The determinant of Matrix 5 is -349, which follows the sequence of determinants (-2, -11, -59, -349). This pattern is maintained across all matrices, ensuring the determinant sequence progresses accordingly.",
    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>Consider a sequence where each term is a matrix transformation result. The sequence of 2×2 matrices follows this pattern:\nMatrix 1: [[1,2],[3,4]] with determinant -2\nMatrix 2: [[2,5],[7,12]] with determinant -11\nMatrix 3: [[5,12],[17,29]] with determinant -59\nMatrix 4: [[12,29],[41,70]] with determinant -349\nEach matrix's elements follow specific relationships to the previous matrix, and the determinants form their own sequence. Identify the pattern governing both the matrix element generation and the determinant sequence, then determine what Matrix 5 should be and verify its determinant fits the pattern.<｜Assistant｜><think>\nTo determine the pattern for Matrix 5, we observe that each subsequent matrix has elements generated by doubling the previous matrix and adding the preceding matrix. This results in Matrix 5 having elements [12, 29] and [41, 70]. The determinant of Matrix 5 is -349, which follows the sequence of determinants (-2, -11, -59, -349). This pattern is maintained across all matrices, ensuring the determinant sequence progresses accordingly.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nWe are given a sequence of 2×2 matrices with determinants forming a sequence. Our goal is to identify the pattern governing the matrix elements and the determinant sequence, then determine Matrix 5 and verify its determinant.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Matrix 1: } & \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\det = -2 \\\\\n\\text{Matrix 2: } & \\begin{bmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 12 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\det = -11 \\\\\n\\text{Matrix 3: } & \\begin{bmatrix} 5 & 12 \\\\ 17 & 29 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\det = -59 \\\\\n\\text{Matrix 4: } & \\begin{bmatrix} 12 & 29 \\\\ 41 & 70 \\end{bmatrix}, \\quad \\det = -349 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n**1. Determining the Pattern for Matrix Elements:**\n\n- **Matrix 1:** \\([1, 2, 3, 4]\\)\n- **Matrix 2:** \\([2, 5, 7, 12]\\)\n- **Matrix 3:** \\([5, 12, 17, 29]\\)\n- **Matrix 4:** \\([12, 29, 41, 70]\\)\n\n**Pattern:**\n- Each element in the next matrix is generated by doubling the corresponding element in the previous matrix and adding the preceding matrix.\n  - \\(2 \\times 1 + 3 = 5\\)\n  - \\(2 \\times 2 + 5 = 9\\) (Wait, but the next element is 5, not 9. This suggests a different pattern.)\n  - \\(2 \\times 3 + 7 = 11\\) (But the next element is 12, which is 11 + 1)\n  - \\(2 \\times 4 + 12 = 20\\) (But the next element is 29, which is 20 + 9)\n  \n  The pattern is not straightforward doubling. Instead, each element is generated by doubling the previous element and adding the preceding element.\n\n**Pattern:**\n- Each element in the next matrix is \\(2 \\times \\text{previous element} + \\text{preceding element}\\).\n\n**2. Determinant Sequence:**\n- \\(-2, -11, -59, -349\\)\n\n**3. Determinant of Matrix 5:**\n- Using the pattern, Matrix 5 will have elements generated by doubling the previous matrix and adding the preceding matrix.\n- Assuming the pattern continues, Matrix 5 will have elements \\([12, 29, 41, 70]\\).\n- The determinant of Matrix 5 is calculated as \\(-349\\).\n\n**4. Verification:**\n- Matrix 5: \\(\\begin{bmatrix} 12 & 29 \\\\ 41 & 70 \\end{bmatrix}\\)\n- Determinant: \\(12 \\times 70 - 29 \\times 41 = 840 - 1189 = -349\\)\n\n**Conclusion:**\n- Matrix 5 is \\(\\begin{bmatrix} 12 & 29 \\\\ 41 & 70 \\end{bmatrix}\\) with a determinant of \\(-349\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{bmatrix} 12 & 29 \\\\ 41 & 70 \\end{bmatrix}}\n\\]",
