  {
    "task_id": "PROB_112",
    "base_task_id": "PROB_112",
    "category": "probabilistic_thinking",
    "instruction": "A quality control system inspects electronic components in three sequential stages. Stage 1 catches 80% of defective items but also incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. Items that pass Stage 1 go to Stage 2, which catches 70% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. Items passing Stage 2 go to Stage 3, which catches 60% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. If the initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, and an item makes it through all three stages to the customer, what is the probability it is actually defective? Also, what percentage of originally good items are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process?",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A quality control system inspects electronic components in three sequential stages. Stage 1 catches 80% of defective items but also incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. Items that pass Stage 1 go to Stage 2, which catches 70% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. Items passing Stage 2 go to Stage 3, which catches 60% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. If the initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, and an item makes it through all three stages to the customer, what is the probability it is actually defective? Also, what percentage of originally good items are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process?<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "To determine the probability that an item, which passes through all three stages, is defective, we start with the initial defect rate of 10%. This means 10% of items are defective, and 90% are good.\n\nFor the defective items, 80% are correctly identified, leaving 20% that are incorrectly rejected. For the good items, 3% are rejected, while 97% remain good. After passing through Stage 1, 80% of defective items and 97% of good items are retained.\n\nStage 2 processes these items, with 70% being caught and 3% rejected. Stage 3 then handles 60% of the defective items and 2% of the good items. By the end of Stage 3, 60% of defective items and 2% of good items are rejected.\n\nThe probability that an item passes all stages is 60% of defective and 2% of good. The probability that an item is defective and passes all stages is 60% of 10%, which is 6%. The total probability of passing is 6% + 2% = 8%. Thus, the probability that a passed item is defective is 6% / 8% = 75%.\n\nFor the second part, 2% of originally good items are rejected, contributing 2% to the total rejection. This represents 2% out of 100% of good items, resulting in a 2% rejection rate.",
    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A quality control system inspects electronic components in three sequential stages. Stage 1 catches 80% of defective items but also incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. Items that pass Stage 1 go to Stage 2, which catches 70% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. Items passing Stage 2 go to Stage 3, which catches 60% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. If the initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, and an item makes it through all three stages to the customer, what is the probability it is actually defective? Also, what percentage of originally good items are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nTo determine the probability that an item, which passes through all three stages, is defective, we start with the initial defect rate of 10%. This means 10% of items are defective, and 90% are good.\n\nFor the defective items, 80% are correctly identified, leaving 20% that are incorrectly rejected. For the good items, 3% are rejected, while 97% remain good. After passing through Stage 1, 80% of defective items and 97% of good items are retained.\n\nStage 2 processes these items, with 70% being caught and 3% rejected. Stage 3 then handles 60% of the defective items and 2% of the good items. By the end of Stage 3, 60% of defective items and 2% of good items are rejected.\n\nThe probability that an item passes all stages is 60% of defective and 2% of good. The probability that an item is defective and passes all stages is 60% of 10%, which is 6%. The total probability of passing is 6% + 2% = 8%. Thus, the probability that a passed item is defective is 6% / 8% = 75%.\n\nFor the second part, 2% of originally good items are rejected, contributing 2% to the total rejection. This represents 2% out of 100% of good items, resulting in a 2% rejection rate.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nWe analyze the quality control process through three stages, with each stage having specific rejection rates. The initial defect rate is 10%, and we aim to determine two probabilities:\n\n1. The probability that an item, passing through all three stages, is defective.\n2. The percentage of originally good items that are incorrectly rejected.\n\n**1. Probability of Defective Item After Three Stages**\n\n- **Stage 1:** \n  - Defective items: 10% of 10% = 1% (caught)\n  - Good items: 90% of 10% = 90% (rejected 3%)\n  \n- **Stage 2:**\n  - Defective items: 80% of 1% = 0.8% (caught)\n  - Good items: 97% of 90% = 97% (3% rejected)\n  \n- **Stage 3:**\n  - Defective items: 60% of 0.8% = 0.48% (caught)\n  - Good items: 98% of 97% = 95.06% (2% rejected)\n  \n- **Total Items Passing Through All Stages:** 0.48% + 2% = 2.48% (defective)\n- **Probability of Defective Item:** \\(\\frac{0.48\\%}{2.48\\%} \\approx 19.5\\%\\)\n\n**2. Percentage of Originally Good Items Rejected**\n\n- **Rejected Good Items:** 2% of 10% = 2%\n- **Percentage of Rejected Good Items:** \\(\\frac{2\\%}{10\\%} = 20\\%\\)\n\n**Final Results:**\n\n- **Defective Items:** 19.5%\n- **Rejected Good Items:** 20%\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{19.5\\% \\text{ defective items and } 20\\% \\text{ of good items rejected}}\n\\]",
    "n_tokens": 762,
    "closed_think": true,
    "hit_token_cap": false,
