    "base_task_id": "SPAT_115",
    "category": "spatial_reasoning",
    "instruction": "A rectangular swimming pool is 25 meters long, 15 meters wide, and 2 meters deep throughout. A lifeguard sits at one corner of the pool at ground level. A swimmer in distress is floating at the water surface at the diagonally opposite corner. The lifeguard can run along the pool edge at 6 meters per second and swim at 2 meters per second. Determine the optimal path for the lifeguard to reach the swimmer in minimum time, considering that the lifeguard must enter the water at some point along the pool's perimeter. Should the lifeguard run all the way around to the opposite corner and then swim, dive in immediately and swim diagonally, or choose some intermediate point to enter the water?",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A rectangular swimming pool is 25 meters long, 15 meters wide, and 2 meters deep throughout. A lifeguard sits at one corner of the pool at ground level. A swimmer in distress is floating at the water surface at the diagonally opposite corner. The lifeguard can run along the pool edge at 6 meters per second and swim at 2 meters per second. Determine the optimal path for the lifeguard to reach the swimmer in minimum time, considering that the lifeguard must enter the water at some point along the pool's perimeter. Should the lifeguard run all the way around to the opposite corner and then swim, dive in immediately and swim diagonally, or choose some intermediate point to enter the water?<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "To determine the optimal path for the lifeguard, we need to evaluate three possible routes: running along the edge to the opposite corner, swimming diagonally, and a path that involves running to an intermediate point on the pool and then swimming to the opposite corner. Each route has a different time, so we calculate the time for each and compare them to find the minimum time.\n\nFirst, the lifeguard runs 25 meters at 6 m/s, taking 3.5 seconds. Swimming diagonally covers 25 meters at 2 m/s, taking 12.5 seconds. The total time is 16 seconds.\n\nFor the intermediate point, the lifeguard runs 25 meters to the point, taking 3.5 seconds, and swims 25 meters, taking 12.5 seconds. The total time is also 16 seconds. Both routes take the same time, so either path is equally efficient.\n\nTherefore, the lifeguard can choose either the straight run and swim path or the intermediate point with running and swimming, both achieving the minimum time of 16 seconds.",
    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A rectangular swimming pool is 25 meters long, 15 meters wide, and 2 meters deep throughout. A lifeguard sits at one corner of the pool at ground level. A swimmer in distress is floating at the water surface at the diagonally opposite corner. The lifeguard can run along the pool edge at 6 meters per second and swim at 2 meters per second. Determine the optimal path for the lifeguard to reach the swimmer in minimum time, considering that the lifeguard must enter the water at some point along the pool's perimeter. Should the lifeguard run all the way around to the opposite corner and then swim, dive in immediately and swim diagonally, or choose some intermediate point to enter the water?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nTo determine the optimal path for the lifeguard, we need to evaluate three possible routes: running along the edge to the opposite corner, swimming diagonally, and a path that involves running to an intermediate point on the pool and then swimming to the opposite corner. Each route has a different time, so we calculate the time for each and compare them to find the minimum time.\n\nFirst, the lifeguard runs 25 meters at 6 m/s, taking 3.5 seconds. Swimming diagonally covers 25 meters at 2 m/s, taking 12.5 seconds. The total time is 16 seconds.\n\nFor the intermediate point, the lifeguard runs 25 meters to the point, taking 3.5 seconds, and swims 25 meters, taking 12.5 seconds. The total time is also 16 seconds. Both routes take the same time, so either path is equally efficient.\n\nTherefore, the lifeguard can choose either the straight run and swim path or the intermediate point with running and swimming, both achieving the minimum time of 16 seconds.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nWe aim to determine the optimal path for the lifeguard to reach the swimmer in the shortest time. The lifeguard can run along the pool edges and swim at a faster rate. We evaluate three possible paths:\n\n1. **Running to Opposite Corner (25 meters)**\n   - **Swim Distance:** 25 meters\n   - **Swim Time:** \\( \\frac{25}{2} = 12.5 \\) seconds\n   - **Total Time:** \\( 3.5 + 12.5 = 16 \\) seconds\n\n2. **Swimming Diagonally**\n   - **Swim Distance:** 25 meters\n   - **Swim Time:** \\( \\frac{25}{2} = 12.5 \\) seconds\n   - **Total Time:** 12.5 seconds\n\n3. **Intermediate Point**\n   - **Running to Intermediate Point:** 25 meters (3.5 seconds)\n   - **Swim to Opposite Corner:** 25 meters (12.5 seconds)\n   - **Total Time:** 16 seconds\n\n**Conclusion:**\nBoth the straight run and swim path and the intermediate point with running and swimming achieve the minimum time of 16 seconds. Therefore, the lifeguard can choose either path efficiently.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{16 \\text{ seconds}}\n\\]",
