  {
    "task_id": "SPAT_116",
    "base_task_id": "SPAT_116",
    "category": "spatial_reasoning",
    "instruction": "A three-dimensional sculpture consists of five identical regular tetrahedra (pyramids with equilateral triangular faces). Each tetrahedron has an edge length of 10 cm. The five tetrahedra are arranged such that they all share a common vertex at the center, and each tetrahedron's opposite face lies flat against a different face of an imaginary regular dodecahedron. If you were to construct the smallest possible sphere that completely encloses this entire sculpture, what would be the relationship between the sculpture's maximum extent from the central vertex and the positions of the outermost vertices? Additionally, how many of the tetrahedra's vertices would actually touch the surface of this enclosing sphere?",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A three-dimensional sculpture consists of five identical regular tetrahedra (pyramids with equilateral triangular faces). Each tetrahedron has an edge length of 10 cm. The five tetrahedra are arranged such that they all share a common vertex at the center, and each tetrahedron's opposite face lies flat against a different face of an imaginary regular dodecahedron. If you were to construct the smallest possible sphere that completely encloses this entire sculpture, what would be the relationship between the sculpture's maximum extent from the central vertex and the positions of the outermost vertices? Additionally, how many of the tetrahedra's vertices would actually touch the surface of this enclosing sphere?<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "To construct the smallest enclosing sphere for the sculpture, the sphere must encompass all five tetrahedra. Each tetrahedron has a base (an equilateral triangle) and a height of approximately 13.693 cm. The sphere must reach from the center to the farthest points of the tetrahedra, which are the top and bottom of each tetrahedron. The sphere's radius is determined by the maximum distance from the center to any point on the sculpture, ensuring all tetrahedra are enclosed.\n\nThe sphere's radius is approximately 13.693 cm, with the top and bottom of each tetrahedron touching the sphere. This setup ensures the smallest possible sphere contains all five tetrahedra. The sphere touches all five tetrahedra, with each has its top and bottom touching the sphere, resulting in 10 outermost vertices.",
    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A three-dimensional sculpture consists of five identical regular tetrahedra (pyramids with equilateral triangular faces). Each tetrahedron has an edge length of 10 cm. The five tetrahedra are arranged such that they all share a common vertex at the center, and each tetrahedron's opposite face lies flat against a different face of an imaginary regular dodecahedron. If you were to construct the smallest possible sphere that completely encloses this entire sculpture, what would be the relationship between the sculpture's maximum extent from the central vertex and the positions of the outermost vertices? Additionally, how many of the tetrahedra's vertices would actually touch the surface of this enclosing sphere?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nTo construct the smallest enclosing sphere for the sculpture, the sphere must encompass all five tetrahedra. Each tetrahedron has a base (an equilateral triangle) and a height of approximately 13.693 cm. The sphere must reach from the center to the farthest points of the tetrahedra, which are the top and bottom of each tetrahedron. The sphere's radius is determined by the maximum distance from the center to any point on the sculpture, ensuring all tetrahedra are enclosed.\n\nThe sphere's radius is approximately 13.693 cm, with the top and bottom of each tetrahedron touching the sphere. This setup ensures the smallest possible sphere contains all five tetrahedra. The sphere touches all five tetrahedra, with each has its top and bottom touching the sphere, resulting in 10 outermost vertices.\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\n1. **Understanding the Sculpture:**\n   - **Tetrahedra:** Five identical regular tetrahedra with edge length 10 cm.\n   - **Arrangement:** All share a common vertex (center), with each tetrahedron's opposite face flat against a different face of a regular dodecahedron.\n\n2. **Enclosing Sphere:**\n   - **Radius Calculation:**\n     - Each tetrahedron has a height of approximately 13.693 cm.\n     - The enclosing sphere must reach from the center to the farthest points (top and bottom of each tetrahedron).\n     - Radius = 13.693 cm.\n\n3. **Outermost Vertices:**\n   - Each tetrahedron has 4 vertices.\n   - All 5 tetrahedra have 20 vertices.\n   - These vertices touch the enclosing sphere.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10 \\text{ vertices touch the sphere}}\n\\]",
