new dependencies are created, and the cascade is affected. Compensating mechanisms include efficient production, controlled costs, quality, sales management, and financial stability. \n\nI should structure this into four points, each with its own subpoints. That should cover all the required areas.\n</think>\n\nThe implementation of a just-in-time (JIT) inventory system in a manufacturing company will have ripple effects across various departments, impacting costs, sales, and cash flow. Here's how this system will propagate through the company and its departments:\n\n1. **Increased Pressure and Risk in Departments:**\n   - **Producers:** Face increased costs due to higher inventory levels.\n   - **Sales:** Sells more inventory, reducing profit margins.\n   - **Quality Control:** Inspects more products, increasing costs.\n   - **Finance:** Serves with excess cash, supporting production.\n   - **Producers:** Face increased production, requiring more resources.\n\n2. **New Dependencies:**\n   - **Producers:** Increase in costs and production.\n   - **Quality Control:** Higher costs and potential bottlenecks.\n   - **Sales:** Reduced profits due to increased inventory.\n   - **Finance:** Excess cash fund for production.\n   - **Producers:** Increased production, supporting finance.\n\n3. **Cascade Effects:**\n   - Disruption in any department (e.g., production) leads to slower production, higher inventory, increased costs, reduced sales, and excess cash.\n   - These effects cascade through the company, impacting all departments.\n\n4. **Compensating Mechanisms:**\n   - Efficient production ensures controlled costs.\n   - Maintains quality control and sales management.\n   - Serves excess cash supports production.\n   - Financial stability ensures sufficient cash for production.\n\nThese mechanisms ensure the JIT system's impact is mitigated, maintaining company stability.",
