and.\n\nCoyotes are mesopredors, so they feed on rabbits. More rabbits mean more coyotes, which are healthy but also deplete grassland. This keeps rabbits around, but grassland is gone, so rabbits can't grow, leading to low populations.\n\nBirds depend on rabbits, so their numbers rise. But with low grassland, birds can't feed, leading to low bird populations. This affects bird health and may reduce their numbers, which in turn affects other species.\n\nGrassland health is impacted by both rabbit and bird populations. Low grassland means less grass, which is bad for the ecosystem. This depletes grassland, which is bad for rabbits and birds, and also affects the grassland itself.\n\nThe feedback loops mean that as grassland decreases, rabbit and bird populations drop, which reduces lion and coyotes, further depleting grassland. This cycle continues, leading to a decline in all species and a degraded grassland.\n\nManagement expects high grassland, so they remove food, leading to low grassland, which depletes rabbits and birds, reducing lion and coyotes, and further depleting grassland. This is bad for the park, so management must remove food to keep grassland healthy.\n\nSo, the feedback loops cause grassland depletion, leading to low rabbit and birds, which reduce lion and coyotes, further depleting grassland. This is bad for the park, so management must remove food to keep grassland healthy.\n\nI need to show how each effect cascades, leading to the depletion of grassland, which depletes rabbits and birds, which reduce lion and coyotes, and further deplete grassland. This creates a cycle, leading to loss of grassland and affected species.\n\nI need to show the feedback loops, which are the cycles that sustain the park, and the negative impact on grassland, which is the feedback loop that depletes the park. The management removes food, which is the positive feedback loop, leading to the negative feedback loop. The negative loop depletes grassland, which feeds back on rabbit and birds, which feed back on lions and coyotes, which deplete grassland. This is a cycle, so management must remove food to prevent the cycle.\n\nI need to show the feedback loops, which are the positive and negative cycles, and the management removes food, which is the positive feedback loop. This leads to the negative feedback loop (depletion of grassland), which feeds back on rabbit and birds, which feed back on lions and coyotes, which deplete grassland. This is a cycle, so management must remove food to prevent the cycle.\n\nI need to show the feedback loops (positive and negative) and the removal of food (positive feedback loop) leading to grassland depletion (negative feedback loop). This feeds back on rabbit and birds, which feed back on lions and coyotes, which deplete grassland. This is a cycle, so management must remove food to prevent the cycle.\n\nI need to show the positive feedback loop (removal of food) leads to negative feedback (depletion of grassland). This feeds back on rabbit and birds, which feed back on lions and coyotes, which deplete grassland. This is a cycle, so management must remove food to prevent the cycle.\n\nI need to show removal of food (positive feedback loop) leads to grassland depletion (negative feedback loop). This feeds back on rabbit and birds, which feed back on lions and coyotes, which deplete grassland. This is a cycle, so management must remove food to prevent the cycle.\n\nI need to show removal of food (positive feedback loop) leads to grassland depletion (negative feedback loop). This feeds back on rabbit and birds, which feed back on lions and coyotes, which deplete grassland. This is a cycle, so management must remove food to prevent the cycle.\n\nI need to show removal of food (",
