llway is narrower, with a smaller area for the ceiling and floor, resulting in a narrower visual field and a shorter perceived length.\n\n**Conclusion**:\nThe architectural feature that explains the perceptual discrepancy is the visual field and its projection onto the hallway. The front view captures a wider visual field, enhancing the length perception, while the back view captures a narrower visual field, reducing the perceived length. This setup creates a visual illusion where the hallway appears longer from the front and shorter from the back, despite having the same physical dimensions.\n\n**Final Answer**: The hallway's visual field and projection from front to back and back to front create a visual illusion where the hallway appears longer from the front and shorter from the back, resulting in the perceptual discrepancy. This is achieved by the visual field extending from the front to the back, with the front having a wider angle and the back having a narrower angle, thereby influencing the perceived length of the hallway.\n</think>\n\nThe architectural feature that explains the perceptual discrepancy in the hallway is the visual field and its projection onto the hallway. The hallway is enclosed with a straight entrance and exit, each equipped with a door. The visual field extends from the front to the back of the hallway, with the front having a wider visual field and the back having a narrower visual field. \n\n- **Front View**: The wider visual field (front to back) enhances the visual field, increasing the perceived length of the hallway (40 feet) and capturing the wider visual field (front to back) with a larger area (40 feet wide). This creates a front view with a wider angle, enhancing the length perception.\n\n- **Back View**: The narrower visual field (south to north) reduces the visual field, capturing the narrower area (40 feet wide) and a smaller visual field (south to north). This results in a shorter perceived length (55 feet) and a narrower visual field, reducing the perceived length.\n\nThe hallway's visual field and projection from front to back and back to front create a visual illusion where the hallway appears longer from the front and shorter from the back, resulting in the perceptual discrepancy. \n\n\\boxed{\\text{Visual Field Projections and Angles}}",
