  {
    "task_id": "PROB_113",
    "base_task_id": "PROB_113",
    "category": "probabilistic_thinking",
    "instruction": "A climate model predicts severe storms based on four independent atmospheric conditions: high humidity (occurs 30% of days), low pressure (occurs 25% of days), high wind shear (occurs 20% of days), and temperature inversion (occurs 15% of days). Historical data shows that when all four conditions are present, severe storms occur 95% of the time. When exactly three conditions are present, storms occur 60% of the time. When exactly two conditions are present, storms occur 25% of the time. When fewer than two conditions are present, storms occur 5% of the time. On a given day, a severe storm occurs. What is the probability that all four atmospheric conditions were present? What is the probability that exactly three conditions were present?",
    "prompt": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A climate model predicts severe storms based on four independent atmospheric conditions: high humidity (occurs 30% of days), low pressure (occurs 25% of days), high wind shear (occurs 20% of days), and temperature inversion (occurs 15% of days). Historical data shows that when all four conditions are present, severe storms occur 95% of the time. When exactly three conditions are present, storms occur 60% of the time. When exactly two conditions are present, storms occur 25% of the time. When fewer than two conditions are present, storms occur 5% of the time. On a given day, a severe storm occurs. What is the probability that all four atmospheric conditions were present? What is the probability that exactly three conditions were present?<｜Assistant｜><think>\n",
    "chain": "To determine the probability of each atmospheric condition combination given the occurrence of a severe storm, we apply Bayes' Theorem. For each condition combination (with 4, 3, 2, and 0 conditions), we calculate the probability of the conditions (P) and the probability of severe storm given the conditions (P(S|C)), then compute P(C|S) = P(C) * P(S|C) / P(S).\n\nFor each condition combination:\n- High humidity (H): P(H) = 0.3, S = 0.95, P(S|H) = 0.95\n- Low pressure (L): P(L) = 0.25, S = 0.60, P(S) = 0.60\n- High shear (S): P(S) = 0.20, S = 0.25, P(S) = 0.20\n- Inversion (I): P(I) = 0.15, S = 0.05, P(I) = 0.05\n\nCalculate P(S) for each condition set:\n- S = 0.3 * 0.95 = 0.285\n- L = 0.25 * 0.60 = 0.15\n- S = 0.20 * 0.25 = 0.05\n- I = 0.15 * 0.05 = 0.005\nTotal P(S) = 0.285 + 0.15 + 0.05 + 0.005 = 0.405\n\nCompute P(C|S) for each condition:\n- H: (0.3 * 0.95) / 0.405 = 0.285 / 0.405 ≈ 0.707\n- L: (0.25 * 0.60) / 0.405 = 0.15 / 0.405 ≈ 0.373\n- S: (0.20 * 0.25) / 0.405 = 0.05 / 0.405 ≈ 0.123\n- I: (0.15 * 0.05) / 0.405 = 0.005 / 0.405 ≈ 0.012\nTotal = 0.707 + 0.373 + 0.123 + 0.012 = 1.000\n\nThus, the probabilities are:\n- 4 conditions: ~70.7%\n- 3 conditions: ~37.3%\n- 2 conditions: ~12.3%\n- 0 conditions: ~1.2%",
    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>A climate model predicts severe storms based on four independent atmospheric conditions: high humidity (occurs 30% of days), low pressure (occurs 25% of days), high wind shear (occurs 20% of days), and temperature inversion (occurs 15% of days). Historical data shows that when all four conditions are present, severe storms occur 95% of the time. When exactly three conditions are present, storms occur 60% of the time. When exactly two conditions are present, storms occur 25% of the time. When fewer than two conditions are present, storms occur 5% of the time. On a given day, a severe storm occurs. What is the probability that all four atmospheric conditions were present? What is the probability that exactly three conditions were present?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nTo determine the probability of each atmospheric condition combination given the occurrence of a severe storm, we apply Bayes' Theorem. For each condition combination (with 4, 3, 2, and 0 conditions), we calculate the probability of the conditions (P) and the probability of severe storm given the conditions (P(S|C)), then compute P(C|S) = P(C) * P(S|C) / P(S).\n\nFor each condition combination:\n- High humidity (H): P(H) = 0.3, S = 0.95, P(S|H) = 0.95\n- Low pressure (L): P(L) = 0.25, S = 0.60, P(S) = 0.60\n- High shear (S): P(S) = 0.20, S = 0.25, P(S) = 0.20\n- Inversion (I): P(I) = 0.15, S = 0.05, P(I) = 0.05\n\nCalculate P(S) for each condition set:\n- S = 0.3 * 0.95 = 0.285\n- L = 0.25 * 0.60 = 0.15\n- S = 0.20 * 0.25 = 0.05\n- I = 0.15 * 0.05 = 0.005\nTotal P(S) = 0.285 + 0.15 + 0.05 + 0.005 = 0.405\n\nCompute P(C|S) for each condition:\n- H: (0.3 * 0.95) / 0.405 = 0.285 / 0.405 ≈ 0.707\n- L: (0.25 * 0.60) / 0.405 = 0.15 / 0.405 ≈ 0.373\n- S: (0.20 * 0.25) / 0.405 = 0.05 / 0.405 ≈ 0.123\n- I: (0.15 * 0.05) / 0.405 = 0.005 / 0.405 ≈ 0.012\nTotal = 0.707 + 0.373 + 0.123 + 0.012 = 1.000\n\nThus, the probabilities are:\n- 4 conditions: ~70.7%\n- 3 conditions: ~37.3%\n- 2 conditions: ~12.3%\n- 0 conditions: ~1.2%\n</think>\n\n**Solution:**\n\nWe use **Bayes' Theorem** to determine the probability of each atmospheric condition combination given the occurrence of a severe storm. The formula for Bayes' Theorem is:\n\n\\[\nP(C|S) = \\frac{P(S|C) \\cdot P(C)}{P(S)}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(C\\) represents the conditions (High humidity, Low Pressure, High Shear, Inversion)\n- \\(S\\) represents the severe storm\n- \\(P(C)\\) is the probability of each condition\n- \\(P(S|C)\\) is the probability of severe storm given each condition\n- \\(P(S)\\) is the total probability of severe storm\n\n**Step 1: Calculate \\(P(S)\\) for each condition combination**\n\n1. **High humidity (H):**\n   \\[\n   P(S|H) = 0.95, \\quad P(H) = 0.3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(S|H) \\cdot P(H) = 0.95 \\times 0.3 = 0.285\n   \\]\n\n2. **Low pressure (L):**\n   \\[\n   P(S|L) = 0.60, \\quad P(L) = 0.25\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(S|L) \\cdot P(L) = 0.60 \\times 0.25 = 0.15\n   \\]\n\n3. **High shear (S):**\n   \\[\n   P(S|S) = 0.25, \\quad P(S) = 0.20\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(S|",
