    "chain": "Okay, so I need to prove that the identity element e is unique, and also that the inverse element a' is unique for each a in M. Let me start by recalling the axioms given.\n\nFirst, the operation ⊙ is associative. That is, for all a, b, c in M, (a ⊙ b) ⊙ c = a ⊙ (b ⊙ c). So, associativity is satisfied.\n\nSecond, there exists an identity element e in M such that for all a in M, e ⊙ a = a ⊙ e = a. So, e is the identity element.\n\nThird, for every a in M, there exists an element a' in M such that a ⊙ a' = a' ⊙ a = e. So, every element has an inverse.\n\nI need to prove that e is unique. Let me think about how to approach this.\n\nI remember that in group theory, the identity element is unique. But since we're given that ⊙ is associative and that e is the identity, I need to show that e is unique.\n\nLet me try to assume that there might be two identity elements, e and e'. So, suppose there are two elements e and e' such that e ⊙ e' = e and e' ⊙ e = e. Wait, but actually, the problem says that e is the identity, so perhaps I need to show that e is the same as e'.\n\nWait, no, the problem says that e is the identity, so I need to show that e is unique. So, perhaps I can proceed as follows.\n\nSuppose that e is the identity element. Let me try to show that e is unique.\n\nWait, perhaps I can use the fact that e is the identity, so for any a in M, e ⊙ a = a. So, perhaps I can use the axioms to show that e is unique.\n\nWait, maybe I can proceed by assuming that there are two identity elements, e and e', and then show that they must be the same.\n\nWait, but the problem says that e is the identity, so perhaps I need to show that e is unique. So, perhaps I can proceed as follows.\n\nWait, perhaps I can use the fact that e is the identity, so let me try to show that e is unique.\n\nWait, perhaps I can proceed by considering that e is the identity, so for any a in M, e ⊙ a = a.\n\nWait, maybe I can use the fact that the inverse of a is unique. Wait, but I'm sup