    "full_text": "<｜begin▁of▁sentence｜><｜User｜>An online platform runs A/B tests for a new feature. In the control group (50% of users), the conversion rate is 8%. In the test group (50% of users), the observed conversion rate is 9.2%. However, the platform knows that 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see, while regular users in the control group convert at 5%. A user converts after seeing the test version. What is the probability this user is a power user versus a regular user? Additionally, what must be the conversion rate of regular users in the test group to produce the observed 9.2% overall conversion rate?<｜Assistant｜><think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out this probability. Let me try to break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me restate the problem to make sure I understand it.\n\nWe have an online platform running A/B tests for a new feature. In the control group, which is 50% of the users, the conversion rate is 8%. In the test group, the observed conversion rate is 9.2%. However, there's a twist: 15% of users in both groups are 'power users' who convert at 25% regardless of which version they see. Regular users in the control group convert at 5%.\n\nSo, the question is: given that a user has converted, what is the probability that this user is a power user? Additionally, we need to find the required conversion rate of the regular users in the test group to produce the observed 9.2% overall conversion rate.\n\nLet me try to structure this.\n\nFirst, let's define the variables.\n\nLet me denote:\n\n- Let me denote the probability that a user is a power user as P(power user). Wait, actually, the problem says that 15% of users in both groups are power users. Wait, no, hold on. Wait, the problem says that 15% of users in both groups are power users. Wait, no, actually, the problem says that 15% of users in both groups are power users. Wait, no, let me read it again.\n\nWait, the problem says: \"1