he cone is \\( 2\\pi r \\), which is \\( 2\\pi \\times 4 = 8\\pi \\) meters.\n\nWhen we unfold the cone, the sector's arc length is equal to the circumference of the base. The arc length of a sector is given by \\( \\theta l \\), where \\( \\theta \\) is the angle in radians. So, the arc length is \\( \\theta l = 8\\pi \\).\n\nWait, no, actually, the arc length is \\( \\theta \\times l \\), but the circumference of the base is equal to the arc length. So, the arc length is \\( 8\\pi \\). Therefore, \\( \\theta = \\frac{8\\pi}{l} \\).\n\nBut wait, I think I might have messed up. Let me think again.\n\nThe circumference of the base is \\( 2\\pi r \\), which is \\( 8\\pi \\). The arc length of the sector is equal to the circumference, so:\n\n\\( \\theta = \\frac{\\text{arc length}}{l} = \\frac{8\\pi}{l} \\)\n\nBut \\( l \\) is the slant height, which is \\( 2\\sqrt{13} \\). So, substituting:\n\n\\( \\theta = \\frac{8\\pi}{2\\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{4\\pi}{\\sqrt{13}} \\)\n\nWait, that seems a bit off. Let me double-check.\n\nWait, no, actually, the arc length is \\( 8\\pi \\), and the slant height is \\( l \\). So, the angle \\( \\theta \\) is \\( \\frac{\\text{arc length}}{l} \\), which is \\( \\frac{8\\pi}{l} \\). So, plugging in \\( l = 2\\sqrt{13} \\), so:\n\n\\( \\theta = \\frac{8\\pi}{2\\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{4\\pi}{\\sqrt{13}} \\)\n\nBut I think I might have made a mistake here because the angle \\( \\theta \\) is actually the angle of the sector, which is the angle between the two radii of the sector. So, the sector's angle is \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\text{arc length}}{l} \\), which is correct.\n\nWait, but the problem is that the angle is in radians. So, the angle is \\( \\theta = \\frac{8\\pi}{l} \\), which is \\( \\frac{8\\pi}{2\\sqrt{13}} = \\frac{4\\pi}{\\sqrt{13}} \\). So, that's the angle.\n\nBut I think I need to find the angle in terms of the sector. So, the angle is \\( \\theta = \\frac{8\\pi}{l} \\), which is \\( \\frac{",
