    "instruction": "A quality control system inspects electronic components in three sequential stages. Stage 1 catches 80% of defective items but also incorrectly rejects 5% of good items. Items that pass Stage 1 go to Stage 2, which catches 70% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 3% of good items. Items passing Stage 2 go to Stage 3, which catches 60% of remaining defective items and incorrectly rejects 2% of good items. If the initial defect rate in the production line is 10%, and an item makes it through all three stages to the customer, what is the probability it is actually defective? Also, what percentage of originally good items are incorrectly rejected by the end of the three-stage process?",
